#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# THE PAGE'S 3D HALF: a model, a camera on it, and a hand (PaintModeler).
#
#     run PaintProvider/scripts/paint_model.etcs model=<PaintModeler> sheet=<Drawable2D> paper=<Drawable2D>
#         bar=<Drawable2D> layers=<Drawable2D> canvas=<PaintSurface> input=<PaintInput>
#         palette=<PaintPalette> brush=<PaintTool> layout=<Layout>
#
# The page stays one page. The camera is a Drawable2D that owns pixels
# (RenderProvider/Camera3D.h), so it goes in the tree where the picture is and
# the switch is which of the two is shown: `model.Toggle()` hides one set of
# nodes and shows the other (PaintModeler::AddPane) and solves the layout
# again. The toolbar row is the same row in both: the 2D bar, or the 3D one
# (paint_model_bar.etcs), whichever is shown -- a hidden box takes no room.
#
# THE MODELLER IS THE BOOT'S, not this script's: the session templates and the
# page's own calls reach boot globals only, so it is made there and handed in.
#
# WHAT IS NOT A PART is made here and is the script's: the floor, the three
# arrows, the camera. What IS one is a node of the pool, under a container of
# its own (paint_model_world.etcs) -- and that container is what an export
# writes, so a file has the model in it and nothing of the room it was made in.

requires model   [PaintModeler]
requires sheet   [Drawable2D]
requires paper   [Drawable2D]
requires bar     [Drawable2D]
requires layers  [Drawable2D]
requires canvas  [PaintSurface]
requires input   [PaintInput]
requires palette [PaintPalette]
requires brush   [PaintTool]
requires layout  [Layout]

# ── the pane and its bar: a model's, as any page has them ───────────────────
#
# Inside the page's own pane (a child of it, for the layout's sake: a box is
# placed relative to its layout parent, and the page's pane is what the
# window's size already arrives at), over the picture; the bar in the toolbar
# row, the same row as the 2D bar -- a hidden box takes no room.
run paint_model_pane.etcs model=model pane=paper row=sheet palette=palette brush=brush layout=layout input=input

# What a part may be made of: a name the model's lines carry (the material
# cell steps through them), for whatever weighs it -- a game's builder has its
# own list, from the game (KartProvider's kart_looks.etcs).
model.AddMaterial(steel)
model.AddMaterial(alloy)
model.AddMaterial(plastic)
model.AddMaterial(wood)
model.AddMaterial(foam)

# The page's pane is RETAINED, so what is composed over it stays in its raster
# after this is hidden -- which is why the modeller is handed the canvas, and
# draws the page again when the model closes.
model.BindSurface(@canvas)

# Hidden with the model: the 2D bar, and the layer window, which is the
# document's and would lie over the camera.
model.AddPane(2, @layers)
model.AddPane(2, @bar)
